{
  "id": "h11",
  "order": 11,
  "title": "Bass note, then chord",
  "summary": "Jump from a low bass note to a chord and back, the left hand's most common job.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "The jump",
      "body": "A lot of music has the left hand play a low note on the beat, then a chord higher up, then back down: bass, chord, bass, chord.\n\nThe move between them is a **hand jump**: the whole hand lifts and lands in a new place, already in the shape of what it will play.\n\n**Why it matters:** jumping chords feel hard when the hand hunts for keys after it lands. Shape the hand in the air, look at where it will land just before you go, and it arrives ready.\n\nPress **Watch**: bass C with the little finger, then the C chord with fingers 5, 3 and 1.",
      "hands": [
